  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 237 PM CDT Thu Aug 6 2026\n\nBroad mid/upper troughing remains in place over the north\ncentral CONUS, with the stationary surface boundary draped from\nNE Missouri into central MO and turning westward south of the KC\nmetro. Showers and storms have finally exited the CWA to the\neast, but overcast skies remain, keeping temperatures in the mid\nto upper 70s for most locations as of 3 PM this afternoon.\nRobust convection has blossomed across SW Missouri in the\nvicinity of Springfield. Can't completely rule out some\nconvective development in the far southern or southeastern\nportions of the CWA later this afternoon/evening along/ahead of\nthe stalled boundary, and SPC has these areas within a marginal\nrisk for severe weather. However, CAM guidance suggests\nconvective activity should remain south and southeast of our\nCWA. Low level jet induced convection is likely to develop\nacross east central Kansas late tonight, but this should by and\nlarge remain west of our CWA.\n\nTemperatures should heat up tomorrow as we become more under the\ninfluence of the 597 dam H5 high over the Four Corners region.\nAnother shortwave trough is progged to descend out of Canada and\ninto the Upper Midwest tomorrow, bringing a cold front north to\nsouth with it, which is likely to trigger convection across \nsouthern Minnesota into northern Iowa by late morning and early \nafternoon. It is possible that this convection could grow\nupscale into an MCS and could potentially stay together and\nreach northern Missouri by tomorrow evening, although CAM\nguidance varies significantly at this time. That being said, SPC\nis concerned enough about this possibility that is has included\nnorthern MO (along and north of US Highway 36) within a marginal\nrisk for severe storms for tomorrow.\n\nAs we head into the weekend and early next week, the western \nridge continues to exert increased influence over our region, \nwith temperatures and heat indices on the rise. Heat indices \nshould approach 100 degrees for southern portions of the CWA on \nSaturday, with heat indices flirting with heat advisory criteria\non Sunday and Monday (and perhaps even into Tuesday). Given our\nposition toward the eastern edge of the primary ridge and \nwithin west northwesterly flow aloft, MCS activity will be\npossible, especially on Saturday night/Sunday morning and again\non Tuesday.",
